\section{Discussion and Limitations}\label{sec:discussion}

In the datasets studied, the number of comparisons that carries a Bradley--Terry conclusion is what the model of the dataset's own graph predicts, to within the margin, wherever there are enough pairs to decide; in standard-error units the datasets look alike, and a confidence interval says little about this number, since separated pairs lose their separation after a small targeted change that random noise of the same size does not approach. The first-order identity behind the scale makes agreement partly expected, and the finding is bounded by the margin and the instances.

\paragraph{What the data cannot say.}
Design is confounded with dataset: the crowd, expert, model-judge and sports data differ in models, prompts, graph and size as well as in who labels. Every statement about a design is therefore a statement about the instances studied. The two matched contrasts (same battles, same questions, different labeller) isolate the labeller on those instances only, and neither supports a general statement about crowds versus experts. The expert instance is small (six models, 80 questions) and its uncertainty wide.

\paragraph{Where the measurement applies.}
The real datasets cover a narrow part of the space of graph density, tie rate and size: the six-model sets are complete graphs, Arena is a dense graph with many models, and tie rates run from none (basketball) to about a third (Arena crowd votes). How fragility depends on density, ties and $N$ is therefore read from the synthetic grid, where the truth is known, and from variation inside a dataset; the real data carry only the claims about whether they sit inside or outside their own same-graph reference. The rule that predicts $k^{*}$ is checked by holding out whole datasets and designs, and the number of units held out is small.

\paragraph{Scope of the estimator and the threat model.}
We study the Bradley--Terry maximum-likelihood leaderboard on pair-aggregated counts with ties as half a win. Elo updates, win rates, Rank Centrality, other regularisation and other judge pipelines are untested. Deletion measures how much of the data carries a conclusion, not an action anyone can take on crowd votes; flips are the effect of labelling error or of an adversary who can change labels; inserting votes of one's choice would cost less than the flips we count and is outside the study. $k^{*}$ is an upper bound found by search, not a proven minimum: where an exact optimum can be computed we compare the search with it (\S\ref{sec:experiments}); at the scale of Arena only the verified upper bound is available.

\paragraph{Practice and impact.}
A leaderboard can report, beside the confidence interval, the number of comparisons that carry a published conclusion in standard-error units, the same number for a Bradley--Terry dataset of the same graph, and the share of separated pairs that survive a stated budget; a count far below the reference would be a misfit worth examining, and none was found beyond the margin. The same numbers could inform someone who wants to manipulate a ranking; we study no attack on a deployed platform and see the measurement as an audit.
